<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12729"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Johno<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Johno<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, April 07, 2005 - 10:24 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Keeping track of serial killers is far from a science. There are figures available but what do they tell us? <BR>Take one example. BTK. The killings take place in modern times in a modern American city. Seven victims linked to most likely the same murderer. This is believed to be a fact for years until 2004 when the figure was raised to eight. In just one year that figure jumped to ten. Will it continue to climb? Very likely. <BR>Now go back in time to a crowded urban English industrial city. Jack the Ripper. In a brief late summer five murders believed to have been linked to the same murder. That figure has stood and we will never know how many more there may have been. <BR>Serial killings are in many cases unreported, unlinked and often covered up. China has only recently admitted that they have had a couple of serial killers since the 1990s. They claim it&#39;s something new there. <BR>There are and historically have been less serial killers operating in countries outside the USA but many of these fewer killers were far more prolific than most of the known USA killers. Fewer killers but more victims per killer. <BR>Third world countries barely report their serial killers. Communist countries try to pretend they don&#39;t exist and modern industrialized crime fighting societies don&#39;t really know what they are dealing with. Almost all captured serial killers have more victims than were originally thought. <BR>Available statistics show that North America has 80% of the world&#39;s known serial killers of the 20th century. <BR>The USA has 76% of the world&#39;s serial killers. <BR>The south is number one followed by the west, the midwest and the northeast. California is the top state. <BR>Europe accounts for 16% with Great Britain number one followed by Germany and France. <BR>Third world countries make up 4%. <BR>BTK represents the modern mobile serial killer. <BR>Jack the Ripper represents the crowded urban inner city serial killer. <BR>Why is the USA number one. We have both. A vastly mobile society with cars and buses and trains and planes and interstates and highways and freeways and parkways and six lane surface streets. We also have crowded inner cities and big sprawling cities. We also have lots of open spaces. We also have the ways, the means and the opportunities. <BR>Icyou says pay attention to your children and that is a large part of the problem. People don&#39;t. <BR>The three most frequent common denominators of serial killers is they come from dysfunctional families, unwed mothers and divorced parents.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
