<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12744"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Phoenix<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Phoenix<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->3-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, April 08, 2005 - 10:38 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->ld:  Thanks for the tip. I&#39;ll read it if I’m not too busy. <BR> <BR>Jo:  WOW.  Can you recommend a bibliography? <BR> <BR>On a funky note, here&#39;s a link to a good footnote on: the summary of: an outline of: the readers digest condensed version of: the cliff-notes over: the introduction to: the chapter in Freshman Psych 101 on psycopathic personalities.  <a href="http://www.dummies.com/WileyCDA/DummiesArticle/id-1217.html" target=_top>http://www.dummies.com/WileyCDA/DummiesArticle/id- 1217.html</a>. <BR> <BR>A link to “The Psychopathy Checklist - Revised &#40;PCL-R&#41; Hare, 1991” full annotated version: <a href="http://www.swin.edu.au/victims/resources/assessment/personality/psychopathy_checklist.html" target=_top>http://www.swin.edu.au/victims/resources/assessmen t/personality/psychopathy_checklist.html</a> <BR> <BR>If Hare is correct, that psychopaths are born not made, then all the pop talk about child abuse or other inappropriate child rearing as a causative factor is out the window.  Hug your kid all you want, but you’re either hugging a psychopath or you’re not.  You can’t blame the parents, or the teachers, or television, or video games. <BR> <BR>Scary stuff. <BR> <BR>Phoenix <BR> <BR> <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12744-->
<!--Post: 12746--><!--Time: 1112983745-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12746"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Steve_huff<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Steve_huff<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, April 08, 2005 - 11:09 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text--><i>&#34;In the time progression of Raders pictures, you can see the change in expression and eyes. Why?&#34;</i> <BR> <BR>I realize this is perhaps a rhetorical question, but I noted very early after his arrest how Rader&#39;s photographs are never the same - I mean, it&#39;s almost eerie, how something seems to shift in each photo that&#39;s been published. <BR> <BR>I&#39;ve noticed this phenomenon in one person I was close to at one time. She <i>&#40;we were engaged to be married at one point&#41;</i> was a wickedly intelligent eldest daughter of a Methodist preacher. She was charming when we met in a sort of retro-hippie way, sexy too. But early in our relationship I noticed this strange mutability in her photographs. One photo could show a beautiful girl with a wide smile and sexy, half-lidded eyes, another show a frumpy, sullen geek girl.  <BR> <BR>Then I saw that this was seen in her behavior as well. To borrow a term from Star Trek, M. <i>&#40;like I would use her real name!&#41;</i> phase-shifted. At church, she was the Reverend&#39;s helpful, kindhearted oldest girl, the lead soprano in the choir, a real angel. In the everyday portion of our relationship - unfortunately we were one of those couples you often encounter in college who for a time become completely subsumed in one another&#39;s personalities - she was often whiny, manipulative, self-absorbed. In the non-everyday portion, even though I was her &#34;first,&#34; she was, somehow, a masochist. That is as delicate as I can put it. To be clear, I am not a sadist or a masochist, so this was a puzzle to me, at first. <BR> <BR>None of this was, in and of itself, that odd. Then, in the third and final year of our relationship, I got sick. For one week, terribly, terribly ill. Nothing I put in my body stayed, and it didn&#39;t come out solid - again, being delicate. I lost 10 pounds, ended up going to the emergency room to get IV fluids because I was dangerously dehydrated.  <BR> <BR>Later that year my breakup with her began, in part because she wanted to add a third person to the relationship, a woman, and I wasn&#39;t all about that, in spite of what you might think about the drives and desires of a typical 22-year-old guy. <i>&#40;I&#39;m 37 now, for perspective&#41;</i>.  <BR> <BR>Long story short, the night we broke up, I felt like I saw the real person for the first time - without going into detail, I gave her reason to be angry aside from just breaking up, it was true - I was culpable, for sure - but that night we were in a car parked in a parking lot near her apartment house, and she exploded. I mean <i>roared</i> at me. At some point she hit me, I&#39;m pretty sure. She was only 5&#39;0&#34; tall, but honestly, I was frightened. <i>&#40;And I&#39;m 6&#39;0&#34; and have lifted weights for most of my adult life.&#41;</i>  <BR> <BR>After the breakup I began to examine the relationship, and I began to realize something about her. She was a sociopath. Not a potential killer, necessarily - her upbringing may have saved society that burden, as she had a sister who turned out to be a very solid, upstanding citizen, a Democratic political activist in her hometown - but I began to recall jokes I&#39;d made all along in a different light. Like when I called M. &#34;my little con artist&#34; because of her ability to very subtly get people to do what she wanted - <i>every time.</i> <BR> <BR>And I thought about that time I got sick. How it wasn&#39;t too long after that she seemed to grow more and more restless in the relationship, leading up to that thing that caused it to end. Yeah, I wondered. She was already pretty computer-savvy - far more than me, with my rockin&#39; n&#39; rollin&#39; ADHD, toodling around on computers in the late 80&#39;s was just about unthinkable. She was one of the first people I ever knew who had AOL, in the early 90&#39;s. I guess I wondered if she&#39;d done any research on poisons.  <BR> <BR>In retrospect, I <b>have</b> concluded that worry about being poisoned was pure paranoia on my part. But it&#39;s telling. No other woman I&#39;ve had a serious relationship with ever gave me reason to truly suspect something that awful. Outwardly, M. didn&#39;t either. But subconsciously, reasons for worry were settling in my mind for the duration of the relationship. All of this gelled for me the day I decided to get rid of all my &#34;memorabilia&#34; from that relationship. I started going through our photographs, the ones she&#39;d given me and the ones we&#39;d taken. She changed, in every single photo. Shifted. Something was always <i>moving</i> under the surface.  <BR> <BR>What does this mean, really? I don&#39;t know. It errs towards the mystical, I admit. But she was a lesson for me in the manifold ways a kind of bland evil can be manifested in a person. She was no serial killer, for sure. I think for a time, in her way, she was sure she loved me. But there was something inside her that was never <i>moored</i>.  <BR> <BR>Who is to say that if she&#39;d been the eldest <b>son</b> of that preacher, if her sexuality was found by her at that point to bound up with violence in some form, that she wouldn&#39;t have, as that postulated male child, been a far, far more dangerous person to be close to? I think the possibility is strong. <BR> <BR>So that strange mutability I see in Rader&#39;s bland gaze from every photo I&#39;ve seen is familiar to me, and telling. Some people are simply missing something. Or they have it, but it is so weak that it never becomes rooted, anchored in their beings.  <BR> <BR>They are the Hollow Men, and Hollow Women. I find answers in poetry - my first published words were poems, so I guess that&#39;s inevitable. In T.S. Eliot&#39;s work I have found a great deal of truth, perhaps truth he didn&#39;t intend. His poem &#34;The Hollow Men&#34; - I recommend it to you strongly for a kind of abstracted insight into what I&#39;m saying. Rader was one of those men, his voice whispering across Wichita like wind in dry grass.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
