<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12759"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Teardrop<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Teardrop<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->7-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Saturday, April 09, 2005 - 7:27 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Manson was a changling, and photos of Bundy hint of the same thing. Rader&#39;s expressions during his public court appearances will be very vital and will reinforce his innocense or guilt in the eyes of the public. I wonder if he is capable of even ingenuine humility. Any defiance on his part will not be helpful.  I do not know why it is, but preachers&#39; kids seem to have a huge number of behavioral issues. I have some former inlaw relatives who have allowed their son to brutalize animals since he was 2 years of age. No intervention on their part. Should his behavior ever become known, his status as a great little league player will come tumbling down. It is a total facade. As he would talk about what he did to animals, his eyes would become dull and far away, his voice monotone, his face without expression: he was a dead man talking. Someone once told me that the easiest lay in the church was the minister&#39;s daughter. Of course, they live a higher profile life and are held to a higher standard; however, some things are basic to all of us, regardless of status in life. <BR> <BR>Sidenote: After being a probation officer and knowing who is cooking, I do not like to eat out unless I am very familiar with the establishment. And, yes, strange bouts of woes could have been a &#39;taint&#39; administered for &#39;one&#39;s own good&#39;.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
