<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12841"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Johno<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Johno<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, April 12, 2005 - 11:10 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Just for the record Angrywolf, Skov is a woman and I too like her posts. At least most of them.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12841-->
<!--Post: 12842--><!--Time: 1113377004-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12842"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><a href="mailto:vscantu1@msn.com" target="_blank"><!--/email--><!--name-->Vscantu<!--/name--></b></a>
</a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Vscantu<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Wednesday, April 13, 2005 - 12:23 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Wow, some pretty amazing &amp; amusing posts on PREVENTING FUTURE BTKs&#39;. Tho&#39; no one has really come up with a proposed way of actually doing it. Not that there necessarily is a right answer. <BR> <BR>One thing that is a major contributor to the high number of serial killers in the U.S. is our punishment system. Right now we have an &#34;ALL YOU CAN KILL&#34; policy. That is, your worst punishment for murdering one single person is either life in prison or &#34;death&#34;. Guess what your punishment is if you kill a 2nd person? And even a 3rd? How &#39;bout murdering 33 people? You guessed it, the same as if you had only killed one! <BR> <BR>This is definitely NOT a deterent to killing multiple humans in the U.S. In fact, it is almost an ENCOURAGEMENT to someone who has this &#34;killer instict&#34;. <BR> <BR>Changing this law would be a good first step towards discouraging future serial killers. Because, if not, they can say &#34;Hey, I killed one person &amp; I&#39;ll probably be caught, so why not kill a few more??&#34; The punishment will be exactly the same. <BR> <BR>Victor<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12842-->
<!--Post: 12848--><!--Time: 1113406248-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12848"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Steve_huff<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Steve_huff<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Wednesday, April 13, 2005 - 8:30 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Victor, this is precisely what I was saying about moving toward a fascist society. What the heck other option is there than life in prison or a death sentence? Killing the entire family line? You see what I mean...any further punishment, because I frankly believe rehabilitation is a joke, would move us over into fascism.  <BR> <BR>For instance, say we decide that the Raders must have know that Denny was up to something. So we arrange for them to be strangled via live video feed to Denny in jail. <BR> <BR>Now, even you who believe they are somehow culpable and have said so in this thread will have to admit that what I just wrote sounds incredibly cruel and horrible.  <BR> <BR>It&#39;s the kind of thing a modern-day Nazi government would do. <BR> <BR>Victor, a question; are you saying that we should grade the penalties? How would that work?<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12848-->
<!--Post: 12850--><!--Time: 1113411263-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12850"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Embersage<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Embersage<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Wednesday, April 13, 2005 - 9:54 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Steve, I read you reviews on Z and Btk today, I posted as well on what a great job you are doing as usual. Which is my point, Number one: Awareness, How to prevent Serial Killers, by doing exactly what we are doing now. Get involved. We went from cops taking notes, to people having video cameras, to the information Internet highway. This board proves that you can kill someone, but eventually someone is going to find out who you are. Aside from that, there are a million other things we can do. Monitor what your kids are doing, take part in their lives. Monitor the level of violence they are allowedto watch. Get to them while they are young. I also think that even though it may not be morally correct. It may be time to start rewiring known killers brain patterns. <!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12850-->
<!--Post: 12851--><!--Time: 1113413453-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12851"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><a href="mailto:bmodule_244@hotmail.com" target="_blank"><!--/email--><!--name-->Bmodule<!--/name--></b></a>
</a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Bmodule<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Wednesday, April 13, 2005 - 10:30 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->It would seem the way to prevent serial killers is to address the cause. Unfortunately, there is no tangible cause. Serial killings are essentially thrill killings. Serial killers kill because they get something personal out of it: sexual, ego trip, membership in the Serial Killers Club, and in cases where they actually are insane, god only knows what. They are just not as consistant as they could be. <BR> <BR>Stiff laws do have a deterent effect to a certain extent. It stops me. But killing is also icky and requires a lot of explaining. Not to mention that it can also lead to retribution. Victor is right though. Once you commit the first killing, the deterent effect of the law is gone. The trick seems to be to arrest them before they kill a whole slough of folks &#40;or at least before 30 years elapses&#41;. <BR> <BR>Since serial killing seems to be a personal achievement thing, and stopping the first one seems unlikely, the trick is to catch them before they can make a serial out of the thing. However until they have committed three murders, there is no way to know they are on a roll &#40;I&#39;m counting two murders only as a repeat offence here&#41;. Quick evidence gathering and good detective work is the key. And good CSI work is essential. <BR> <BR>The first order of business here, I think, should be for the FBI or National Association of Homicide Detectives, or Federal Bureau of Crime Scene Investigators or someone to lay down a set of rules to follow upon discovering a murder, and educate the public &#40;including children, since half the bodies discovered seem to be found by a boy and his dog taking a shortcut&#41;. If regular people &#40;who discover most murders initially&#41; knew how to keep from contaminating the crime scene, i.e. walk a straight line to body, check pulse, and retrace steps, phone cops, keep people out until the get there &#40;and hope they don&#39;t mess things up&#41;, then it would be easier to establish a serial. There is probably a prefered way to check the pulse, and other things to do and not to do, and of course if the victim is obviously dead &#40;wormy,etc.&#41;, stay away. Things like using the phone in the room where the body is, washing the dead body germs off of your hands in the closest sink, picking up the gun-knife-axe-blunt instrument, turning on the[more] lights, straightening up, flushing the toilet &#40;even using the toilet&#41;, vomiting on the body, or in the immediate crime scene area, are all the kinds of things which can destroy evidence.   <BR> <BR>The second order of business would seem &#40;to me anyway&#41; to be getting rid of all of the rules pertaining to Modus Operendi. [Serial killers take vacations]. If two murders occur in a time span or an area, the police should start WITH the assumption that they are related, if they aren&#39;t, that will shake out later. The danger is in overlooking a serial,thus wasting time and letting evidence disappear or be destroyed. If the killings are not related, then they can still catch one of them &#40;and that will narrow the search for the other by reinforcing the assumption that there is a tangible motive&#41;. <BR> <BR>Third order of business: keep the public appraised &#40;release information&#41;. For instance, during the Otero murders, BTK&#39;s letter proved that he didn&#39;t remember a lot of the facts about the crime &#40;A WHOLE LOT OF FACTS&#41; but he stole a radio &#40;or it was blaring&#41;, a watch and a keychain. That is three things that he would know &#40;and remember, since he still had them&#41; so why keep the rest secret from the public. It certainly didn&#39;t help prevent a panic &#40;the rumors were twice as grusome as the truth&#41;. If an accurate description of the crime scene was proof that the muderer was actually there, then BTK was no where near the place [compare the crime scene description and details of the autopsy in Beattie&#39;s book with BTK&#39;s letter...not even close]. And if the public had known initially that a serial killer was on the loose, he might not have taken the next victims by surprise, or someone might have noticed him knocking on doors, or paid attention to the barking dogs. <BR> <BR> <BR>And finally the fourth order of business: After releasing a description of the murderer, the police should go to Park City and look around for themselves, because in that particular area it seems that if it looks like a duck...and quacks like a duck...and waddles like a duck...and swims like a duck...and has feathers like a duck...and attends the duck club meetings...it&#39;s just the dog catcher.  <BR> <BR>Fortunately, for humanity, serial killing is on the down-swing, and the younger generation seems to be opting for mass-murder, sometimes followed by suicide, over the drawn out and time consuming process of killing each victim one by one. This has been attributed to many factors such as violent movies and video games, overuse of phychotropic medications at an early age, and the proliferation of automatic weapons in the school yard, higher stress on school kids, and the increase of broken families. I feel, though, that there is too much emphasis being placed on efficiency. <BR> <BR>Although easier to solve, the crime still leaves an unacceptably high body count. Plus it is much harder to intervene before the toll is taken. All in all it is not much of an improvement.  <BR> <BR>The one thing that both serial killings and mass murders have in common is that they all seem to stem from competitiveness, which of course is the basis of &#34;American Capitalist Society&#34;. The &#34;point&#34; of the killings seems to stem from the need to &#34;leave a mark&#34; or &#34;leave a legacy&#34; which of course, is what all good capitalists must do to be successfull. And, with the one-ups-manship that is inheirant in competition, each killer , in order to claim their share of infamy, must out-do the preceeding one. <BR> <BR>Ergo, I forsee, in the not too distant future, where things will escalate to serial mass murder in our nation&#39;s Kindergartens. Let&#39;s just hope they don&#39;t start shooting old people...at least before we can have a chance to die of natural causes &#40;like terrorism,car wrecks and botched tummy tucks&#41;.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12851-->
<!--Post: 12856--><!--Time: 1113440972-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12856"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Angrywolf<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Angrywolf<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Wednesday, April 13, 2005 - 6:09 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->More punishment laws won&#39;t stop serial killers..they either believe they won&#39;t ever be caught or they won&#39;t care....The key is early detection..by parents, school teachers, etc etc so they can be treated while still children..if a danger to society maybe there needs to be a way to confine them indefinitely..which is what we need to be doing to pedophiles..because there is no cure...<!--/Text--></td></tr>
