<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12930"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Teardrop<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Teardrop<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->7-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Sunday, April 17, 2005 - 6:57 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->An Egyptian father saw his daughter and a girlfriend speaking to a man on the street. He drowned her in the family swimming pool. I guess that the rest of her siblings got the message. It is not vigilante justice. They believe in the honor system, and do not violate under penalty of death. I would walk the straight and narrow. It is cultural. The US has gotten soft on crime. As a result more are becoming criminal at an earlier age. The best age to kill is while a youth, as one cannot be held as accountable anymore. The poor babies: he did not make the soccer team or she did not get to be cheerleader. The rage built up and they exploded. The Zoloft or Paxil made them do it. All that money and prestige was just too much for him and he was driven to drugs and alcohol. She thought that her mother never accepted her, and the compulsive housekeeping made her freak out. Teardrop lost it because if all those bunnies: she robbed the bank to pay for the pellets and greens and neutering. And on and on..... At some point in time, excuses cannot be a defense for actions. The action must stand alone and the actor held responsible. <!--/Text--></td></tr>
