<!--Post: 13150--><!--Time: 1114312829-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST13150"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><a href="mailto:williammichael@direcway.com" target="_blank"><!--/email--><!--name-->Oklahoma_mike<!--/name--></b></a>
</a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Oklahoma_mike<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->11-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Saturday, April 23, 2005 - 8:20 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Re: A Clockwork Orange; I had the good fortune to meet the author of the book, Anthony Burgess, at a lecture one time.  He related the conditioning ideas in the story had been tried &#40;though not necessariy in that exact form&#41; in the early 1960&#39;s in England, but so far as he knew it was only one or two voluntary subjects and the results were &#34;mixed&#34;. <BR>Schizophrenia certainly tends to run in families but so far no exact genetic component has been found.  My experience working a a state hospital makes me suspect what we call schizophrenia is probably a number of very similar but still different biochemical imbalances, with different mixes of nervous system hormones.  For example, dialysis does not help all schizophrenia, but about 10-15% show definite improvement.  Way back about 1982 I had a patient who needed dialysis and her schizophrenic symptoms all disappeared! <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 13150-->
<!--Post: 13159--><!--Time: 1114409042-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST13159"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><a href="mailto:vscantu1@msn.com" target="_blank"><!--/email--><!--name-->Vscantu<!--/name--></b></a>
</a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Vscantu<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Sunday, April 24, 2005 - 11:04 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->TAC said, in the &#34;Quick note added from Denny 4-20&#34; thread &#40;under &#34;General&#34;&#41;; <BR> <BR>&#34;Unfortunately for the Rader&#39;s of the world we do not have the knowledge to rehabilatate them. Like a child molester or rapist, the fact remains that if they are released they are a solid bet to offend again. . .&#34; <BR>&#34;I believe that we will not be able to help these people until we unlock more of the mysteries of the brain.&#34; <BR> <BR>&#34;Until that point our society needs to acknowledge that we can NOT rehab these lost souls.&#34; <BR> <BR>&#34;Instead of rehabilitation we must use a &#34;substitute&#34; for them. I believe that virtual reality technology and robotics hold the key to substituting the stimulus these offenders crave. . .Creating a robotic/doll or object could act as a outlet for a pedephile or rapist when the &#34;urge&#34; hits. Virtual reality technology could create a &#34;video&#34; game that allows the same type of &#34;outlet&#34;.&#34; <BR> <BR>TAC: Good brainstorming, but I feel that creating robotic dolls or virtual realities for these offenders to indulge their fantasies is a step in the wrong direction. It would probably encourage them to practice their &#34;craft&#34; even more. No, we need something different, but definitely beyond what the U.S. legal system has now. We&#39;ve all heard the &#34;Nothing will ever work.&#34; routine. That leaves it all up to us creative thinkers to help solve this problem. <BR> <BR>We could actually be making history with what we suggest as a solution.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 13159-->
<!--Post: 13160--><!--Time: 1114439177-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST13160"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Sidewalker<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Sidewalker<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->1-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Monday, April 25, 2005 - 7:26 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Huummmmmmmm........ <BR>where to start. <BR>I dont think you can come up with a &#42;recipe&#42; to create a serial killer. Meaning, you could not MAKE someone kill people, just as I dont think you could come up with a way to prevent people from killing people, just like I think you couldnt MAKE someone gay. <BR>I see a difference between the person who &#42;snaps&#42; and kills a bunch of people, what some refer to &#42;going postal&#42; and the type like Rader, Bundy, etc. <BR>They are all mentally ill, for one. But I also dont think that can be an excuse, or a &#42;pass&#42;  <BR>So I dont really know if there IS a way to prevent these monsters. <BR> <BR>an aside......the first I heard of Ted Bundy, was a story in Readers Digest. It caught my attention because the first page of the story had maybe 20 pictures of a man. They all looked different. VERY different. The &#42;headline&#42; to the story was to the effect.......What do these men have in common. <BR>and down low on the page......was..&#42;they are all the same man&#42; <BR>It showed how Bundy could, with little effort change his appearance almost completely. <BR>just thought I would toss that into the mix.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 13160-->
<!--Post: 13188--><!--Time: 1114494870-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST13188"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Angrywolf<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Angrywolf<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Monday, April 25, 2005 - 10:54 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I think we&#39;ve strayed a little bit off the topic..My fault actually ..telling you about my email friend who is schizophrenic..I just recall some brain scans I saw years ago that were allegedly of schizophrenics and were abnormal..and I wondered if BTK&#39;s could be abnormal..Actually,do we know if the brain of any serial killer, Bundy,JohnWayne Gacy, even Jeffrey Dahmer..if any brains of those kinds of people have been examined..either by PET or CT scanning, or physically examined ? Yeah like Sculley in the X-files did, weighing brains to see if they were the right size.Do we know if any of that has been ever done during an autopsy of a serial killer ?Heck have any serial killers ever been autopsied ? <BR> <BR>Obviously if they scan BTK and his brain shows a physical abnormality then I would think they would have to consider an insanity defense...Only thing it would probably mean though..and I don&#39;t know the laws in Kansas..there is innocent by reason of insanity in some states..and guilty but insane in others..in the latter rather than spend the rest of their lives in jail they spend the rest of their lives in a mental institution...Only a few miles from where I am now is one of our states institutions..It&#39;s called Lakeshore..they take adults there who try to commit suicide and evaluate them..the institution for juveniles is called Peninsula..Most people who come to Lakeshore to get evaluated are released after a day or two..the only people they keep locked up are the criminally insane...I talked to a coworker who applied for a job there earlier this evening..this was a few years back..and the person who interviewed her for the job let her know there were really some sick and nasty individuals incarcerated there...Maybe that mental institution where Hannibal Lecter was fictionally incarcerated has its own counterparts with us today... <BR> <BR>I do have to challenge something that was said earlier..I interned at the American Red Cross many years ago..and as far as I know..there is nothing in the brain of schizophrenic people that can infect other people..if there were it would be tested for to prevent it from making other people schizo..The only things they test for are the obvious things..HIV aka Aids,Hep B and Hep C..occasionally other diseases if there is an outbreak...but as long as a person is healthy and hasn&#39;t engaged in  risky sex, drug abuse, tatooing, stuff like that....they will accept blood from you... <BR> <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
